</div>
<div id="lstnumberx6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6</span>
            
            
          <span id="lstnumberx6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">    </span><span id="lstnumberx6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_comment ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">//<span id="lstnumberx6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>Execute<span id="lstnumberx6.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>the<span id="lstnumberx6.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>command<span id="lstnumberx6.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>using<span id="lstnumberx6.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>the<span id="lstnumberx6.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>username<span id="lstnumberx6.2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>as<span id="lstnumberx6.2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>input</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7</span>
            
            
          <span id="lstnumberx7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">    </span><span id="lstnumberx7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_comment ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">//<span id="lstnumberx7.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>**<span id="lstnumberx7.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>The<span id="lstnumberx7.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>code<span id="lstnumberx7.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>generated<span id="lstnumberx7.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>below<span id="lstnumberx7.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>**</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8</span>
            
            
          <span id="lstnumberx8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">    </span><span id="lstnumberx8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;"><span id="lstnumberx8.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">s</span>printf(command,<span id="lstnumberx8.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>"id<span id="lstnumberx8.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>%s",<span id="lstnumberx8.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>username);</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9</span>
            
            
          <span id="lstnumberx9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">}</span>
</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>An example of vulnerable code generation. Line 8 is generated by CodeLlama-7B.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S2.F2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:344.9pt;height:0pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-1.7pt,0.0pt) scale(1.0,1.0) ;"><img src="2510.07189v1/overview_fig1.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/192;" width="476" height="192" alt="Refer to caption">
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>An overview of Secure-Instruct.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Improving LLMs for Better Code Generation.</span>
Acquiring an effective and high-quality fine-tuning dataset from real-world sources is often expensive and labor-intensive. To address this challenge, researchers have explored methods for synthesizing datasets using large language models (LLMs). Recent advancements have introduced synthetic data generation techniques during fine-tuning to address data acquisition challenges and enhance LLM performance on specific tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Babbar and Schölkopf, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Long et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gilardi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abay et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Self-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2022)</a></cite> introduced an automated approach for instruction tuning dataset generation using LLM.
Subsequent works extend this strategy to a broader range of models and tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Taori et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, including code generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chaudhary (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Evol-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2023</a>)</cite> uses ChatGPT to generate diverse instruction-following examples using carefully crafted prompts.
Wei et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2024)</a></cite> proposed OSS-Instruct, a method that uses ChatGPT to generate diverse coding problems and solutions from seed code snippets from open-source code, to create a high-quality fine-tuning dataset to improve code generation accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Improving the Security of LLM Generated Code.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To address security problems in LLM-generated code, Li et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024b)</a></cite> proposed an approach to fine-tune one LLM by using the vulnerability-fixing commits. Hajipour et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hajipour et al., 2024</a>)</cite> introduced a technique to synthesize secure code examples guided by CodeQL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">CodeQL, 2024</a>)</cite> oracle. Another work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> explored the effect of parameter-efficient fine-tuning techniques in secure code generation. He et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">He and Vechev, 2023</a>)</cite> introduced SVEN, an advanced prompting method, i.e., prefix-tuning, that uses property-specific continuous vectors (prefixes) to guide LLMs to generate secure code and reduce the likelihood of generating vulnerable code. The training of SVEN optimizes these continuous vectors using a carefully curated, high-quality dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The most recent work, SafeCoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, proposes a security-focused instruction tuning approach that learns from vulnerable–fix code pairs mined from 145 million commits. Through semi-automated filtering, the authors curate a dataset of 465 high-quality code examples. Their findings show that the LLM fine-tuned on this data achieves state-of-the-art performance in secure code generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Despite improved results, SafeCoder has several limitations. First, the quality of code from open-source repositories is inconsistent, often involving complex dependencies with external libraries. As a result, many code snippets are unsuitable for fine-tuning, requiring significant manual effort to filter and curate appropriate samples. Second, it is challenging to obtain a sufficiently large fine-tuning dataset for each category of security issues (e.g., CWE). For example, the work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite> identified 71 code snippets for CWE-079 but only one for CWE-732, resulting in an imbalance that hinders the model’s ability to achieve robust security performance. Besides, this approach is expensive. They only collected 456 code snippets covering 23 CWEs from 145 million commits, making it difficult to expand to other CWEs.
To address these limitations, we propose Secure-Instruct to automatically construct a more extensive and diverse dataset to further enhance the security of LLM-generated code.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>The Methodology of Secure-Instruct</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce Secure-Instruct, a framework that leverages LLMs (e.g., ChatGPT and Claude) to synthesize fine-tuning data for instruction-tuning code language models (CLMs) to enhance secure code generation.
Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Background and Related Work ‣ Prompt, Synthesize, Fine-Tune: A Secure Code Generation Recipe" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows an overview of Secure-Instruct.
It adopts two data-generation schemes, secure-code driven (Secure-Instruct<sub id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub>) and vulnerable-code driven (Secure-Instruct<sub id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(vul, secure)</span></sub>), to generate high-quality secure training data with no human intervention (Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Synthetic Data Generation By Prompting LLMs ‣ 3 The Methodology of Secure-Instruct ‣ Prompt, Synthesize, Fine-Tune: A Secure Code Generation Recipe" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).
It then uses LLMs to generate natural-language instructions that describe the functionality of each code example in the dataset. Finally, Secure-Instruct applies two instruction tuning strategies to produce security-enhanced LLMs (Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Instruction Tuning LLMs for Secure Code Generation ‣ 3 The Methodology of Secure-Instruct ‣ Prompt, Synthesize, Fine-Tune: A Secure Code Generation Recipe" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>): standard instruction tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Longpre et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and security-specialized instruction tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>CWE Seeds Collection</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we collect CWE documents from the MITRE. The MITRE is an official website that serves as an authoritative resource for understanding and analyzing Common Weakness Enumerations (CWEs). Each CWE entry on the website includes a comprehensive description, highlighting the nature of the vulnerability, its potential impact, and common scenarios in which it arises. Additionally, the entries feature illustrative code examples that contrast vulnerable and secure implementations, offering both context and practical insights.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 CWE Seeds Collection ‣ 3 The Methodology of Secure-Instruct ‣ Prompt, Synthesize, Fine-Tune: A Secure Code Generation Recipe" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents an example of CWE-78 (OS Command Injection) from the MITRE website. This example includes a detailed overview of the weakness, an explanation of its root causes, and multiple code snippets demonstrating how this vulnerability can manifest in real-world software. These examples not only facilitate a deeper understanding of the security flaws but also serve as valuable references for LLMs to generate secure code examples. In particular, Secure-Instruct leverages the information from the MITRE website for each CWE, namely 1) the category of CWE, 2) the overall description, and 3) code snippet examples with their explanations.
In total, we collected 44 CWEs from MITRE.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.07189v1/mitre_webpage.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/328;" width="452" height="328" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>An example of CWE-78 on the MITRE website.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Synthetic Data Generation By Prompting LLMs</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given seed CWE documents, Secure-Instruct employs two data generation strategies to prompt LLM for generating fine-tuning datasets, namely vulnerable-code driven (Secure-Instruct<sub id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(vul, secure)</span></sub>) and secure-code driven (Secure-Instruct<sub id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Secure-Instruct<sub id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(vul, secure)</span></sub></span>. As Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Background and Related Work ‣ Prompt, Synthesize, Fine-Tune: A Secure Code Generation Recipe" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates, vulnerable-code driven data generation includes four steps: (1) prompting an LLM (e.g., GPT 4o) to generate vulnerable code given one CWE information; (2) verifying
that the LLM-generated code indeed contains the specified CWE; (3) prompting LLMs to fix the vulnerabilities and produce secure code; and (4) confirming the secure code is vulnerability-free. Vulnerable-code driven generation ensures the success of generating both vulnerable and secure code for specific CWEs.
We employ two powerful LLMs for code generation, i.e., ChatGPT (gpt-4o-2024-08-06) and Claude (claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022), to generate intentionally vulnerable code snippets. Below, we show the prompt templates for generating and fixing vulnerable code.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="319.67" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 467.83 319.67" width="467.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,319.67) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 313.76 C 0 317.02 2.64 319.67 5.91 319.67 L 461.92 319.67 C 465.18 319.67 467.83 317.02 467.83 313.76 L 467.83 5.91 C 467.83 2.64 465.18 0 461.92 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 313.76 C 1.97 315.94 3.73 317.7 5.91 317.7 L 461.92 317.7 C 464.1 317.7 465.86 315.94 465.86 313.76 L 465.86 5.91 C 465.86 3.73 464.1 1.97 461.92 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 16.2)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.17em;--ltx-fo-height:20.94em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="292.11" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 289.69)" width="458.98"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.17em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Prompt Template for Vulnerable Code Generation of Secure-Instruct<sub id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(vul, secure)</span></sub>.<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Task:<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Please generate an easily understandable vulnerable code snippet based on the following description of CWE. (Please consider the diversity of the vulnerable code examples for each generation.)</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">CWE:<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{&lt;CWE_OVERALL_DESCRIPTION&gt;}</span></span></span></span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A Vulnerable Code Example:<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
This is <span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{&lt;LANGUAGE&gt;}</span> language example.</span></span></span><pre id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.5" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
{&lt;CODE&gt;}
</pre>
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Explanation of the Example:<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{&lt;EXPLANATION&gt;}</span></span></span></span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Can you generate a vulnerable code example for <span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_medium">{&lt;TARGET_LANGUAGE&gt;}<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> </span></span>language?<span id="S3.SS2.p3.pic1.1.7.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="242.95" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 467.83 242.95" width="467.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,242.95) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 237.05 C 0 240.31 2.64 242.95 5.91 242.95 L 461.92 242.95 C 465.18 242.95 467.83 240.31 467.83 237.05 L 467.83 5.91 C 467.83 2.64 465.18 0 461.92 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 237.05 C 1.97 239.22 3.73 240.99 5.91 240.99 L 461.92 240.99 C 464.1 240.99 465.86 239.22 465.86 237.05 L 465.86 5.91 C 465.86 3.73 464.1 1.97 461.92 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 30.38)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.17em;--ltx-fo-height:14.37em;--ltx-fo-depth:1.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="215.4" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 198.79)" width="458.98"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.17em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Prompt Template for Fixing Vulnerable Code of Secure-Instruct<sub id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(vul, secure)</span></sub><span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Task:<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Please fix the vulnerable code based on the following description of CWE :</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">CWE:<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{&lt;CWE_OVERALL_DESCRIPTION&gt;}</span></span></span></span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The Vulnerable Code:<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span><pre id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.5" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
{&lt;GENERATED_VULNERABLE_CODE&gt;}
</pre>
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Can you FIX the code for<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{&lt;LANGUAGE&gt;}</span> </span>? Please make sure the code is secure to the CWE mentioned above and runnable. <span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.6.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For each pair of “CWE-ID” and “programming language”, Secure-Instruct<sub id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(vul, secure)</span></sub> takes both the CWE description and a randomly selected code example from the seed corpus and prompts each LLM to generate 10 vulnerable code snippets.
In total, Secure-Instruct<sub id="S3.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(vul, secure)</span></sub> collected 1,560 code snippets spanning 78 CWE-language pairs.
Then, Secure-Instruct<sub id="S3.SS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(vul, secure)</span></sub> utilizes static security analyzers to verify the vulnerabilities present in the generated code.
Finally, we instruct the LLMs to fix the vulnerable code and verify that the vulnerabilities are gone using static security analyzers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Since static analysis tools can produce both false positives and false negatives, we use two static security analyzers, CodeQL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">CodeQL, 2024</a>)</cite> and SonarQube <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">SonarQube, 2025</a>)</cite>, to complement each other in verification.
A code snippet is considered vulnerable only if both tools produce identical detection results.
However, while CodeQL supports all 78 CWE-language pairs, SonarQube only supports a subset of 41 pairs (i.e., 6 CWEs for C, 9 for Java, 12 for Python, 9 for JavaScript, 0 for Ruby, and 5 for Go).
For the cases not supported by SonarQube, Secure-Instruct solely relies on CodeQL.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">After verification of the 1,560 snippets by static analyzers, 475 code snippets were confirmed to be truly vulnerable. These verified snippets then served as input seeds for the LLMs to generate five fixes for each snippet. Finally, static analyzers are used again to verify whether the generated fixes were valid.
In the end, Secure-Instruct<sub id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(vul, secure)</span></sub> collected 856 pairs of vulnerable code and their corresponding fixed code, which cover 25 CWEs in 6 programming languages, i.e., 39 CWE-language pairs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Secure-Instruct<sub id="S3.SS2.p8.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub></span>. Secure-Instruct<sub id="S3.SS2.p8.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(vul, secure)</span></sub>’s double verification process results in many LLM-generated code snippets being filtered out, e.g., LLMs may fail to generate vulnerable code or fix vulnerable code in many cases, leading to a limited set of secure code snippets.
To source an even larger set of secure code snippets, we propose an alternative approach, Secure-Instruct<sub id="S3.SS2.p8.1.3" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub>. Instead of generating vulnerable code and then repairing it, we directly instruct LLMs to produce secure code using CWE documents as prompts. For each CWE-language pair, we generate 100 secure code snippets that pass verification by both CodeQL and SonarQube, ensuring they do not contain vulnerabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Instruction Tuning LLMs for Secure Code Generation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Secure-Instruct utilizes LLMs to generate a problem description as an “instruction” for each of the fine-tuning datasets.
Then Secure-Instruct employs two instruction tuning methods, i.e., 1). Standard instruction tuning and 2). Secure instruction tuning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generating Instruction.</span>
We follow the work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to construct the instruction for each code snippet. The simplified prompt template is shown below:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS3.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="89.83" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 467.83 89.83" width="467.83"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,89.83) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 83.92 C 0 87.18 2.64 89.83 5.91 89.83 L 461.92 89.83 C 465.18 89.83 467.83 87.18 467.83 83.92 L 467.83 5.91 C 467.83 2.64 465.18 0 461.92 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 83.92 C 1.97 86.09 3.73 87.86 5.91 87.86 L 461.92 87.86 C 464.1 87.86 465.86 86.09 465.86 83.92 L 465.86 5.91 C 465.86 3.73 464.1 1.97 461.92 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 16.89)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.17em;--ltx-fo-height:4.28em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.23em;font-size:10pt;" height="62.27" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 59.15)" width="458.98"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS3.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.17em;">
<span id="S3.SS3.p3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Create a single, very short (maximum two sentences) non-detailed functionality description that could be used as a prompt to generate either of the code snippets below…</span></span>
<span id="S3.SS3.p3.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">{&lt;The_Non-vulnerable_code_snippet&gt;}<span id="S3.SS3.p3.pic1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">
</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">After that, we format the fine-tuning datasets by putting the code snippet to the <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>response<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> tag and the generated instructions to the <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>instruction<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> tag, following a prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Taori et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Standard Instruction Tuning.</span> This process fine-tunes an LLM on instruction-response pairs to improve its ability to follow natural language instructions. It uses supervised fine-tuning (SFT), where the model learns by minimizing loss over the provided dataset. The loss of standard instruction tuning is defined as Equation (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.3 Instruction Tuning LLMs for Secure Code Generation ‣ 3 The Methodology of Secure-Instruct ‣ Prompt, Synthesize, Fine-Tune: A Secure Code Generation Recipe" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), where <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{&lt;i}=(y_{1},y_{2},\dots,y_{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{&lt;i}=(y_{1},y_{2},\dots,y_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the tokens before <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This negative log-likelihood loss penalizes the model when it assigns low probability to the correct next token. It has been adopted by many prior studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\text{std}}(x,y)=-\sum_{i=1}^{|y|}\log P\!\left(y_{i}\mid y_{&lt;i},x\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>std</mtext></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mi>log</mi><mpadded style="width:0.611em;" width="0.611em"><mi>P</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\text{std}}(x,y)=-\sum_{i=1}^{|y|}\log P\!\left(y_{i}\mid y_{&lt;i},x\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Secure-Specialized Instruction Tuning.</span>
SafeCoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite> proposed a specialized fine-tuning technique for secure code generation by leveraging pairs of vulnerable code and fixed code mined from open-sourced software repositories. Since we collected 856 pairs of vulnerable and from Secure-Instruct<sub id="S3.SS3.p7.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(vul, secure)</span></sub>, we applied this instruction tuning approach to our dataset.
As shown in Equation (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.3 Instruction Tuning LLMs for Secure Code Generation ‣ 3 The Methodology of Secure-Instruct ‣ Prompt, Synthesize, Fine-Tune: A Secure Code Generation Recipe" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), the secure loss focuses on
encouraging the model to produce secure implementations, where <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\text{sec}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>sec</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\text{sec}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a binary mask that identifies
whether the <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th token in <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is security-critical using diff between secure and vulnerable code snippets.
This masking ensures that the model receives a strong
positive learning signal specifically on tokens that
contribute to secure coding practices. Complementary to the secure loss, the vulnerability loss, shown in Equation (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.3 Instruction Tuning LLMs for Secure Code Generation ‣ 3 The Methodology of Secure-Instruct ‣ Prompt, Synthesize, Fine-Tune: A Secure Code Generation Recipe" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>),
discourages the model from generating insecure tokens where <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\text{vul}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>vul</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\text{vul}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> marking
vulnerability-inducing tokens. By penalizing the likelihood
of these insecure tokens, the model is pushed away from
vulnerable code generation. Together, <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\text{sec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>sec</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\text{sec}}</annotation></semantics></math> provides a
<span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">positive</span> signal for secure tokens, while
<math id="S3.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\text{vul}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>vul</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\text{vul}}</annotation></semantics></math> provides a
<span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">negative</span> signal against vulnerable ones, which enables SafeCoder to promote secure code generation.
Prior research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">He and Vechev, 2023</a>)</cite> highlights a critical concern: exclusively using secure code examples for fine-tuning can lead to a decline in code functionality. To address this limitation, we follow the work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite> via the integration of a secure code dataset and a functional code dataset during fine-tuning. By using standard instruction tuning for the functional dataset and secure instruction tuning for the secure dataset to improve both functional and secure performance for LLMs. Specifically, we incorporate the functional code dataset from the Evol-Instruct dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, a high-quality dataset designed to enhance the functional robustness and generalization of code generation models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\text{sec}}(x,y,d)=-\sum_{i=1}^{|y|}d_{i}^{\text{sec}}\cdot\log P\!\left(y_{i}\mid y_{&lt;i},x\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>sec</mtext></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>sec</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>log</mi><mpadded style="width:0.611em;" width="0.611em"><mi>P</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\text{sec}}(x,y,d)=-\sum_{i=1}^{|y|}d_{i}^{\text{sec}}\cdot\log P\!\left(y_{i}\mid y_{&lt;i},x\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{\text{vul}}(x,y,d)=-\sum_{i=1}^{|y|}d_{i}^{\text{vul}}\cdot\log\big(1-P(y_{i}\mid y_{&lt;i},x)\big)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>vul</mtext></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>vul</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{\text{vul}}(x,y,d)=-\sum_{i=1}^{|y|}d_{i}^{\text{vul}}\cdot\log\big(1-P(y_{i}\mid y_{&lt;i},x)\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Evaluation Setup</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe our experimental setup.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subject Large Language Models.</span>
We evaluate Secure-Instruct to improve four representative small and medium-sized LLMs with 1 to 7 billion parameters: CodeGen2-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nijkamp et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, CodeLlama-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roziere et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, Mistral-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and StarCoder-1B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. These models are specifically designed for code generation, offering a balance between computational efficiency and performance. These models are widely used in prior works on secure code generation, such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">He and Vechev, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for a fair comparison.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">- CodeGen2-7B</span> is a language model specifically designed for program synthesis. Trained on the “The Stack” dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kocetkov et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, a comprehensive collection of diverse code repositories, CodeGen2 excels at generating and completing code snippets across multiple programming languages.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">- CodeLlama-7B</span> is developed by Meta, builds upon the Llama 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite> architecture and is further fine-tuned specifically for code generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">- StarCoder-1B</span> is trained on “The Stack 1.2” dataset, a collection of code data and natural language text from GitHub, including over 80 programming languages, and the text extracted from GitHub issues and commits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">- Mistral-7B</span> is developed by Mistral AI. It adopted Group-query attention (GQA) and sliding window attention (SWA) to increase the speed and lower the costs. It outperforms other models of the same size on a variety of benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Details on Fine-Tuning LLMs.</span>
We use the parameter-efficient fine-tuning technique, LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2021)</a></cite>, to fine-tune the 7B LLMs. LoRA fine-tuning reduces the GPU memory usage during fine-tuning and maintains a promising performance. For the 1B model, we apply full-weight fine-tuning. All fine-tuning experiments are conducted on five Nvidia RTX A6000 GPUs with 48GB of memory. For full-weight fine-tuning, we set the learning rate to 2e-5, while for LoRA fine-tuning, the learning rate is 3e-4. Each model is fine-tuned in 2 epochs. In LoRA fine-tuning, we configure the LoRA alpha to 16 to control the scaling of LoRA layers and set the dropout rate to 0.05.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Benchmarks.</span>
We evaluate Secure-Instruct using two secure-code generation benchmarks: (1) <span id="S4.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CWEBench</span> crafted and extended by us, and (2) <span id="S4.p8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CWEval</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which enables validation of both functional correctness and security by test cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">First, we craft <span id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CWEBench</span> by extending prior benchmarks for secure code generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siddiq and Santos, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pearce et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Such prior benchmarks are composed of security-specific scenarios and are sourced from three resources, i.e., MITRE documentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">CWE-MIRTE, 2022</a>)</cite>, CodeQL repository <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">CodeQL, 2024</a>)</cite>, and scenarios manually crafted by the authors.
We first combined all three existing benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siddiq and Santos, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pearce et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, removed duplicates, and excluded the scenarios derived from MITRE examples, as Secure-Instruct uses MITRE documents as seeds for LLMs to generate fine-tuning datasets.
Then, we followed a similar process as prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al. (2024)</a></cite> to design 36 new security-specific scenarios covering 27 CWEs by adapting examples from the CodeQL repository.
In the end, our crafted <span id="S4.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CWEBench</span> consists of 93 security-specific scenarios covering 44 CWEs in six languages.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Second, we include a recent benchmark <span id="S4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CWEval</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which uses test cases for automated verification of both <span id="S4.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">functional correctness</span> and <span id="S4.p10.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">security</span> without relying on static security checkers. In addition, <span id="S4.p10.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CWEval</span> provides manual-crafted comprehensive specifications that describe complex user intentions and logic, which means it is a much closer step to real-world applications.
In total, <span id="S4.p10.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CWEval</span> consists of 119 security scenarios covering 31 CWE types in five programming languages.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">De-duplication between fine-tuning datasets and evaluation benchmarks<span id="S4.p11.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span>
To prevent data leakage between the fine-tuning dataset and the two benchmark datasets, we performed de-duplication between training and evaluation following  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
Specifically, we used MinHash with Locality-Sensitive Hashing (LSH) to detect potential duplicates. Code snippets were grouped based on similarity, and pairs with a Jaccard similarity above 0.7 were flagged as potential duplicates. After that we removed these flagged snippets from the fine-tuning dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Research Question Results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present our three RQs. For each RQ, we detail the motivation, approach, and results.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">RQ1: To what extent can Secure-Instruct enhance the capabilities of LLMs to generate secure code?</h3>

<div id="S5.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motivation.</span>
We assess LLMs’ capabilities to generate secure code after being fine-tuned by Secure-Instruct. <span id="S5.SSx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Approach.</span>
For each of the four studied LLMs, we experimented with two settings of Secure-Instruct: (1) applying security-specialized instruction tuning on the dataset of pairs of vulnerable and secure code, i.e., from Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx1.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(vul, secure)</span></sub>; and (2) applying standard instruction tuning on the secure code snippets from Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx1.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub>. Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ RQ1: To what extent can Secure-Instruct enhance the capabilities of LLMs to generate secure code? ‣ 5 Research Question Results ‣ Prompt, Synthesize, Fine-Tune: A Secure Code Generation Recipe" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the statistics of the resulting fine-tuning datasets using Secure-Instruct.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <span id="S5.SSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CWEBench</span>, we define a <span id="S5.SSx1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Secure Ratio</span> to measure LLM’s capability to generate secure code. <span id="S5.SSx1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Secure Ratio</span> of each security scenario is the percentage of secure code among all the generated code. The secure ratio is defined in Equation (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.3 Instruction Tuning LLMs for Secure Code Generation ‣ 3 The Methodology of Secure-Instruct ‣ Prompt, Synthesize, Fine-Tune: A Secure Code Generation Recipe" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) where <math id="S5.SSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of all generated code snippets and <math id="S5.SSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Sec}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Sec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Sec}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> evaluates to true if snippet <math id="S5.SSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is identified as non-vulnerable according to a static analyzer. Thus, the secure ratio quantifies the proportion of generated code that is free from vulnerabilities. To ensure robust results, we generated 100 code snippets for each security-specific scenario. A piece of LLM-generated code is deemed <span id="S5.SSx1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure code</span> if CodeQL detects no vulnerabilities in the code.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Secure Ratio}=\frac{\left|\{s\in S\mid\text{Sec}(s)\}\right|}{|S|}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Secure Ratio</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mtext>Sec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Secure Ratio}=\frac{\left|\{s\in S\mid\text{Sec}(s)\}\right|}{|S|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SSx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SSx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SSx1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CWEval</span> comes with three metrics, namely <span id="S5.SSx1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Func@k</span>, <span id="S5.SSx1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sec@k</span> and <span id="S5.SSx1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Func-Sec@k</span> to evaluate the security and functionality of generated code. In this study, we set k as 1. Specifically, <span id="S5.SSx1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Func@1</span> measures the percentage of security scenarios whose top-1 generated code is functionally correct (i.e., passes all functional test cases). <span id="S5.SSx1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sec@1</span> is defined as the percentage of security scenarios whose top-1 solution is secure (i.e., passes all security test cases). <span id="S5.SSx1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Func-Sec@1</span> concerns both functional correctness and security. Given <math id="S5.SSx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> generated candidates, the unbiased Pass@k is computed by Equation (<a href="#S5.E5" title="In RQ1: To what extent can Secure-Instruct enhance the capabilities of LLMs to generate secure code? ‣ 5 Research Question Results ‣ Prompt, Synthesize, Fine-Tune: A Secure Code Generation Recipe" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. It defines the unbiased Pass@<math id="S5.SSx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> metric, denoted as <math id="S5.SSx1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Metric}_{\theta}@k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Metric</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Metric}_{\theta}@k</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SSx1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in\{\text{Sec},\text{Func},\text{Func-Sec}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mrow><mtext>Sec</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>Func</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>Func-Sec</mtext></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in\{\text{Sec},\text{Func},\text{Func-Sec}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="S5.SSx1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> generated candidates, the metric computes the probability that at least one of the top-<math id="S5.SSx1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> candidates satisfies the evaluation criterion <math id="S5.SSx1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S5.SSx1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Metric}_{\theta}@k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Metric</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Metric}_{\theta}@k</annotation></semantics></math> calculate the percentage of generated outputs that pass test cases under the chosen evaluation criterion within the top-<math id="S5.SSx1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SSx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Metric}_{\theta}@k\;=\;1-\frac{\binom{n-c_{\theta}}{k}}{\binom{n}{k}}\hskip 20.00003pt\theta\in\{\text{Sec},\text{Func},\text{Func-Sec}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>Metric</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.498em">=</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><mtext>Sec</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>Func</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>Func-Sec</mtext><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Metric}_{\theta}@k\;=\;1-\frac{\binom{n-c_{\theta}}{k}}{\binom{n}{k}}\hskip 20.00003pt\theta\in\{\text{Sec},\text{Func},\text{Func-Sec}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S5.T1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:352.9pt;height:56.1pt;vertical-align:-25.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-19.6pt,3.1pt) scale(0.9,0.9) ;">
<table id="S5.T1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
