</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">RQ2: How is Secure-Instruct compared with the state-of-the-art (SOTA) approach in securing LLM-based code generation?</h3>

<div id="S5.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motivation.</span>
SafeCoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite> represents the state-of-the-art in secure LLM-based code generation. It employs an automated pipeline to mine vulnerable code and corresponding fixes from a large corpus of open-source commits (145 million), followed by human intervention for post-processing and validation. The final dataset consists of (instruction, vulnerable code, secure code) triples used to fine-tune LLMs. In contrast, our proposed approach, Secure-Instruct , is a fully automated technique with zero human intervention. Given these fundamental differences, it is important to compare the performance of Secure-Instruct against SafeCoder to assess the effectiveness and scalability of our method.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Approach.</span>
We further conducted a comparative analysis between Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx2.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub> and SafeCoder on CWEBench and CWEval.
In particular, we used the two released models, i.e., CodeLlma-7B and Mistral-7B, which were already fine-tuned by SafeCoder.
In CWEBench, there are 44 CWE types. However, the training dataset of SafeCoder does not include all of them.
SafeCoder was fine-tuned on a dataset containing 23 CWEs, whereas Secure-Instruct was fine-tuned on a dataset covering 44 CWEs.
There are 20 CWEs covered by both Secure-Instruct and SafeCoder.
We used the same configuration as described in the SafeCoder paper, namely a temperature of 0.8 and 100 samples for each security-specific scenario.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SSx2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ RQ1: To what extent can Secure-Instruct enhance the capabilities of LLMs to generate secure code? ‣ 5 Research Question Results ‣ Prompt, Synthesize, Fine-Tune: A Secure Code Generation Recipe" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows that Secure-Instruct achieves higher Func-Sec@1 on CWEval than SafeCoder for both CodeLlama‑7B and Mistral‑7B.
Notably, SafeCoder exhibits a performance drop on CWEval.
For example, SafeCoder’s CodeLlama-7B achieves only 7.1% Func-Sec@1, which is lower than the pre-trained model.
A similar decline is observed in SafeCoder’s Mistral-7B.
These findings are consistent with the observations reported in the study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ RQ1: To what extent can Secure-Instruct enhance the capabilities of LLMs to generate secure code? ‣ 5 Research Question Results ‣ Prompt, Synthesize, Fine-Tune: A Secure Code Generation Recipe" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> breaks down the detailed results on CWEBench of CodeLlama-7B and Mistral-7B in their pre-trained, SafeCoder, and Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub>, on each CWE in our evaluation benchmark. The highest secure ratio for each CWE is highlighted in bold.
In the scenarios whose CWEs are covered in both approaches (the top section of the table), our analysis shows that CodeLlama-7B fine-tuned with Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx2.p4.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub> achieved the highest overall secure ratio (70.6%), outperforming SafeCoder by 2.3% in secure ratio, Mistral-7B fine-tuned with Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx2.p4.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub> achieved 68.9% secure ratio, with a 3.7% higher than SafeCoder.
In the generalization evaluation (i.e., with the full security-specific scenarios of our benchmark), Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx2.p4.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub> demonstrates strong generalization capabilities across a broader range of CWEs for both CodeLlama-7B and Mistral-7B. Specifically, CodeLlama-7B with Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx2.p4.1.5" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx2.p4.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub> significantly outperforms SafeCoder, achieving a 9.2% improvement, while Mistral-7B achieves a 6.0% increase.
At the CWE category level, CodeLlama‑7B fine‑tuned with Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx2.p4.1.6" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx2.p4.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub> achieves the highest or ties for highest secure ratio in 29 of 44 CWEs, outperforming both its pretrained baseline and the SafeCoder‑fine‑tuned variant. Similarly, Mistral‑7B with Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx2.p4.1.7" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx2.p4.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub> leads in 25 of 44 CWEs relative to its pretrained and SafeCoder‑fine‑tuned counterpart.
<span id="S5.SSx2.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">These results demonstrate that Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx2.p4.1.8.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx2.p4.1.8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub> significantly enhances secure code generation, outperforming the SOTA approach, SafeCoder</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SSx2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To assess statistical significance, we conducted Fisher’s exact tests comparing the number of secure code snippets generated by the pre-trained model and the model fine-tuned with Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx2.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub> for each CWE. CWEs with statistical significance (p<math id="S5.SSx2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>0.05) are highlighted in bold, such as CWE-089.
Notably, more than half of the CWEs in Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx2.p5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub> exhibit statistical significance, further demonstrating the robustness of our approach in enhancing secure code generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SSx2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SSx2.p6.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="168.02" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 168.02" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,168.02) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#333333;" fill="#333333" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 162.11 C 0 165.37 2.64 168.02 5.91 168.02 L 471.47 168.02 C 474.73 168.02 477.38 165.37 477.38 162.11 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 162.11 C 1.97 164.28 3.73 166.05 5.91 166.05 L 471.47 166.05 C 473.65 166.05 475.41 164.28 475.41 162.11 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.44 14.13)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:32.85em;--ltx-fo-height:10.29em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="145.14" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 142.45)" width="454.55"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SSx2.p6.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:32.85em;">
<span id="S5.SSx2.p6.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SSx2.p6.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Summary of RQ2:<span id="S5.SSx2.p6.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Safecoder encounters a degradation in CWEval benchmark. Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx2.p6.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx2.p6.pic1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub> significantly outperforms SafeCoder with 9.2% and 6.0% improvements across CWEBench. Even for those CWEs that are part of SafeCoder’s fine-tuning dataset, Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx2.p6.pic1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx2.p6.pic1.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub> achieves 2.3% and 3.7% higher secure ratios in CodeLlama-7B and Mistral-7B, respectively.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">RQ3: Can the LLM fine-tuned by Secure-Instruct preserve the functional correctness in code generation?</h3>

<div id="S5.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motivation.</span>
A critical concern in fine-tuning LLMs in secure code generation is understanding how fine-tuning impacts the functionality of the generated code. While fine-tuning with secure code examples aims to improve the security aspects of the output, it may compromise the model’s ability to produce functionally correct code.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SSx3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Approach.</span>
The benchmark, CWEval, evaluates the functionality of generated code by test cases with the metric Func@1. Besides, we evaluate the fine-tuned models on HumanEval and MBPP, two widely used benchmarks for assessing code generation in Python.
For each task of HumanEval and MBPP, we generate 40 samples using both pre-trained and fine-tuned models and assess their functionality using the Pass@k metric, including Pass@1 and Pass@10.
Pass@k is a standard metric for evaluating code generation, which represents the probability that at least one of the <math id="S5.SSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> generated candidates for a given problem is correct.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SSx3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ RQ1: To what extent can Secure-Instruct enhance the capabilities of LLMs to generate secure code? ‣ 5 Research Question Results ‣ Prompt, Synthesize, Fine-Tune: A Secure Code Generation Recipe" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents the Func@1 results for all models. <span id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">All models, except for StarCoder-1B, have improvements after using Secure-Instruct</span>. CodeLlama-7B has an over 10% increase in Func@1 compared with the pre-trained model. Table <a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ RQ3: Can the LLM fine-tuned by Secure-Instruct preserve the functional correctness in code generation? ‣ 5 Research Question Results ‣ Prompt, Synthesize, Fine-Tune: A Secure Code Generation Recipe" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> presents the Pass@1 (Pass@10 is available in our replication package) on the HumanEval and MBPP benchmarks for pre-trained LLMs, fine-tuned LLMs by SafeCoder and Secure-Instruct.
For CodeLlama‑7B, among the pretrained, SafeCoder‑fine‑tuned, Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(vul, secure)</span></sub>‑fine‑tuned, and Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub>‑fine‑tuned variants, Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub>-fine-tuned model achieves the highest Pass@1 on HumanEval at 35.6%.
For Mistral‑7B, the Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(vul, secure)</span></sub>‑fine‑tuned model achieves the highest score on MBPP at 30.7%.
Overall, most models show performance improvements on both datasets, except for StarCoder-1B. On CWEval, its Func@1 dropped from 15.3% to 13.8% with Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.7" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(vul, secure)</span></sub>, and 12.7% with Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.8" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub>. On HumanEval, the Pass@1 score of StarCoder-1B dropped from 11.7% to 10.9% with Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.9" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">secure</span></sub>and from 11.7% to 6.7% with Secure-Instruct<sub id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.10" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S5.SSx3.p3.1.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(vul, secure)</span></sub>. This decline may be due to the smaller size of StarCoder-1B compared to the other LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SSx3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SSx3.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="101.6" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 101.6" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,101.6) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#333333;" fill="#333333" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 95.69 C 0 98.95 2.64 101.6 5.91 101.6 L 471.47 101.6 C 474.73 101.6 477.38 98.95 477.38 95.69 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 95.69 C 1.97 97.87 3.73 99.63 5.91 99.63 L 471.47 99.63 C 473.65 99.63 475.41 97.87 475.41 95.69 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.44 14.13)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:32.85em;--ltx-fo-height:5.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="78.72" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 76.03)" width="454.55"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SSx3.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:32.85em;">
<span id="S5.SSx3.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SSx3.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Summary of RQ3:<span id="S5.SSx3.p4.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Secure-Instruct enhances secure code generation without sacrificing functional correctness. Across diverse LLMs and benchmarks, Secure-Instruct consistently outperforms both pre-trained LLMs and those fine-tuned by SafeCoder in generating functionally correct code.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
